<section class="hero"><div class="wrap">
<div><p class="kick">Fashion, worked out</p>
<h1>A wardrobe is a system, not a collection.</h1>
<p class="lead">Stylora is about the parts of dressing well that are arithmetic rather than taste: how many pieces you actually need, which colours sit together, and where a hem should fall on you.</p>
<p class="row"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Use the three tools</a> <a class="btn gh" href="capsule-wardrobe-guide.html">Capsule guide</a></p></div>
<figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1791975364944-75ff8830f3b9?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&q=75" alt="Clothing store interior with mannequins in knitwear" width="760" height="1013" fetchpriority="high"></figure>
<div class="inside"><h2>What is inside</h2>
<ol><li>A capsule count built from your week, not a magic number</li>
<li>Colour pairings with the ones to avoid</li>
<li>Hem, sleeve and skirt lengths from your height</li>
<li>Two long guides, no shopping links</li>
<li>Nothing sponsored, ever</li></ol></div>
</div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">The idea</p><h2>Most wardrobes fail on compatibility, not quantity</h2>
<p>The usual problem is not too few clothes. It is a drawer of pieces that each go with one other thing, so a full wardrobe produces four outfits.</p></div>
<div class="strip2">
<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1645171889206-20267ceaa061?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="A close view of two buttons on a blue jumper" width="700" height="438" loading="lazy">
<div><h3>Every piece should cross</h3><p>A top that works with one skirt is worth a fraction of a top that works with all five bottoms. When you are deciding whether to keep something, count the outfits it unlocks rather than how much you like it on the hanger.</p>
<p>That single test does more for a wardrobe than any number of pieces, and it is why the capsule builder here reports outfits alongside the count.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="strip2" style="margin-top:clamp(16px,2.6vw,30px)">
<div><h3>Colour is the other half</h3><p>A consistent base across bottoms, shoes and outerwear is what lets tops be chosen for how much you like them rather than for what they match. Get that right and the rest of the wardrobe stops needing to be planned.</p>
<p>Three colours in an outfit is the ceiling. Past that, nothing is carrying the eye and everything is competing.</p></div>
<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1788181619284-de8bac9d18be?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="Water droplets on a textured fabric surface" width="700" height="438" loading="lazy">
</div></div></section>
<section class="tools" id="tools"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Free tools</p><h2>Three tools, no sign-up</h2>
<p>All three run in your browser. Nothing is stored and nothing is sent anywhere.</p></div>
<div class="tr" id="capsule"><h3>Capsule Wardrobe Builder</h3><p>How many pieces per category your week actually needs, how many outfits that produces, and which base colour holds it together.</p>
<div class="ctl">
<div class="fld"><label for="cpLife">Your week</label><select id="cpLife"><option value="mixed" selected>Mixed, some of both</option><option value="office">Office or formal</option><option value="casual">Mostly casual</option><option value="creative">Creative or relaxed workplace</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="cpClim">Climate</label><select id="cpClim"><option value="four" selected>Four clear seasons</option><option value="mild">Mild all year</option><option value="hot">Hot most of the year</option><option value="cold">Cold most of the year</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="cpBase">Base colour</label><select id="cpBase"><option value="navy" selected>Navy</option><option value="black">Black</option><option value="grey">Grey</option><option value="camel">Camel</option><option value="olive">Olive</option></select></div>
</div>
<div class="out" id="cpOut"></div></div>
<div class="tr" id="colour"><h3>Colour Pairing Guide</h3><p>Pick a colour you own a lot of, and see what sits with it, what fights it, and why.</p>
<div class="ctl two">
<div class="fld"><label for="clBase">Colour</label><select id="clBase"><option value="navy" selected>Navy</option><option value="camel">Camel</option><option value="charcoal">Charcoal</option><option value="olive">Olive</option><option value="burgundy">Burgundy</option><option value="white">Soft white</option></select></div>
</div>
<div class="out" id="clOut"></div></div>
<div class="tr" id="length"><h3>Length Finder</h3><p>Trouser, skirt and sleeve lengths worked from your height and the shoes you will wear with them.</p>
<div class="ctl">
<div class="fld"><label for="lnH">Height (cm)</label><input id="lnH" type="number" min="130" max="215" value="168"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="lnHeel">Heel height (cm)</label><input id="lnHeel" type="number" min="0" max="14" value="2"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="lnCut">Trouser break</label><select id="lnCut"><option value="slight" selected>Slight break</option><option value="none">No break</option><option value="full">Full break</option></select></div>
</div>
<div class="out" id="lnOut"></div></div>
</div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Long reads</p><h2>Two guides, written once and kept current</h2></div>
<div class="gpair">
<article><h3>The Capsule Wardrobe Guide</h3><p>Starting from what you already own, the compatibility test that decides what stays, how many pieces each category really needs, building around a base colour, what to do about the pieces that only work with one thing, and how to keep a capsule from turning into a uniform.</p>
<p><a class="btn gh" href="capsule-wardrobe-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></article>
<article><h3>The Colour Guide</h3><p>Why warmth matters more than shade, choosing a base that your shoes and coats already agree with, the three-colour ceiling, using texture as contrast, what shop lighting hides, and the pairings that look wrong for reasons nobody can name.</p>
<p><a class="btn gh" href="colour-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></article>
</div></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Questions</p><h2>What people ask</h2></div>
<div class="fq"><div><h3>How many pieces does a capsule wardrobe actually need?</h3><p>Somewhere between 25 and 40 for one season, depending on how different your working days and your free days are. The number matters far less than whether every bottom works with every top, which is what turns 20 pieces into 60 outfits instead of 20.</p></div><div><h3>Do I have to throw everything else out?</h3><p>No, and the versions of this idea that say so are the reason it has a bad name. A capsule is the set you reach for without thinking. Everything else can stay in a different part of the wardrobe, and most people find they stop wearing it on their own.</p></div><div><h3>What should the base colour be?</h3><p>Whatever most of your shoes and outerwear already are, because those are the expensive pieces to replace. Navy, charcoal, black, camel and olive all work; the choice matters much less than being consistent about it.</p></div><div><h3>How many colours should an outfit have?</h3><p>Three at most: a base that covers the largest areas, a second that carries the interest, and something small that is neither. Beyond three it stops reading as a decision and starts reading as whatever was clean.</p></div><div><h3>Where should trousers actually finish?</h3><p>At the top of the shoe, with one soft fold at the front, measured in the shoes you will wear with them. Heel height changes this by centimetres, which is why a hem that looks right in flats looks short in heels.</p></div><div><h3>Is it worth having clothes altered?</h3><p>Almost always, and it is the single thing that most changes how a garment looks. A hem, a sleeve and a waist take in cheaply, and a moderately priced garment that fits beats an expensive one that does not.</p></div><div><h3>How do I stop a capsule becoming a uniform?</h3><p>Leave two or three slots deliberately empty, and keep a small set of things that change an outfit without changing the pieces: a scarf, a belt, a second pair of earrings. The base stays constant so these read as decisions.</p></div></div></div></section>
